"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"][25]={"university_electrical_engineering_en:M25-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M25-L1 – Sampling and Reconstruction: Recognising Aliasing in the Time and Frequency Domains<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor–Master Bridge: Transition to Digital Signal Processing and Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Bachelor–Master Bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>,\nFourier fundamentals.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate sampling frequencies,\nalias frequencies and reconstruction conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic self-test<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>What frequency does the sampled sequence of a 650 Hz cosine have at\na sampling frequency of 500 Hz? 2. Can 650 Hz and 150 Hz be\ndistinguished after digitisation? 3. How does quantisation differ from\nsampling? 4. Is a fast FFT already a method for preventing\naliasing?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Answers:<\/strong> 1. 150 Hz in the baseband. 2. Not without\nadditional information. 3. Sampling discretises time, while quantisation\ndiscretises amplitude. 4. No; the FFT analyses values that have already\nbeen sampled. If uncertain, revisit <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L3<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sampling model<\/h2>\n<p>A continuous signal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis ideally measured at times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nT_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the time interval and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=1/T_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe sampling frequency. The sequence is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=x(nT_s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a cosine,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[n]=A\\cos(2\\pi f_0n/f_s+\\varphi).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The digital angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=2\\pi f_0/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of rad/sample. Frequencies differing by integer multiples of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nproduce the same complex exponential sequence for the same phase. For\nreal cosine sequences, the sign of the frequency is also ambiguous if\nthe phase is correspondingly reversed. The signed baseband frequency can\nbe calculated using<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mod<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a=((f_0+f_s/2)\\bmod f_s)-f_s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a zero-phase cosine, the observed positive frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f_a|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Example:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=500<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nms. Both 150 Hz and 650 Hz yield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(0.6\\pi n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>650<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>500<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi\\cdot650/500=2\\pi+0.6\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA cosine at 350 Hz also yields 150 Hz if its phase is zero. At 250 Hz,\nonly alternating values are obtained; a sine wave of the same frequency\nand zero initial phase produces only zeros. The Nyquist frequency is\ntherefore not a robust target for a real measurement.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000218_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Sampling two cosine frequencies at 500 Hz, with identical samples for 150 and 650 Hz.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sampling two cosine frequencies at 500\nHz, with identical samples for 150 and 650 Hz.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spectral perspective and reconstruction<\/h2>\n<p>Mathematically, ideal sampling corresponds to multiplication by a\nDirac comb. With the Fourier convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(f)=\\int x(t)e^{-j2\\pi ft}\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sampled impulse signal yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_s(f)=f_s\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}X(f-kf_s).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If the signal is band-limited to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f|\\le B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s&gt;2B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe copies do not overlap. An ideal low-pass filter with passband gain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a suitable cut-off frequency restores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(f)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the time domain, this corresponds to interpolation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sinc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}x[n]\\,\\operatorname{sinc}(t/T_s-n),\\qquad \\operatorname{sinc}(u)=\\frac{\\sin(\\pi u)}{\\pi u}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The ideal filter cannot be implemented directly as a causal filter.\nFinite data, limited filter order, clock jitter and quantisation\nintroduce additional errors. An analogue anti-aliasing filter is used\n<strong>before<\/strong> the ADC. A subsequent digital filter cannot\nreliably separate interference that has already folded into the signal\nband.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dimensioning the measurement chain<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement is intended to capture 0–180 Hz. The minimum condition\nfor an ideally band-limited signal is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>360<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s&gt;360<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz. At 500 Hz, there is a 70 Hz transition band up to the Nyquist\nfrequency. A higher rate of 1 kHz allows a 320 Hz transition band and\nsimplifies the analogue low-pass filter, but increases the data rate and\ncomputational load. The actual filter requirement is determined by\ntolerable residual interference and expected input spectra, not by the\nNyquist criterion alone. If the signal is subsequently decimated to 500\nHz, a digital anti-aliasing filter must account for the new limit before\nevery second sample is discarded.<\/p>\n<p>A 12-bit ADC with a range of 0–3.3 V has a step size of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4096<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.806<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta=3.3/4096\\approx0.806<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmV under ideal uniform quantisation. At 1 kHz, storing the samples in\n16-bit words produces 2000 bytes/s without protocol overhead. Both\nquantities are independent of aliasing but form part of the measurement\nchain specification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>A 780 Hz interference signal is sampled at 1 kHz.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>220<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_a=-220<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz, cosine at 220 Hz.<\/li>\n<li>A signal has a highest frequency of 3.2 kHz. Is 6 kHz sufficient?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> No, more than 6.4 kHz is required for the\nideally band-limited signal. A practical design accounts for the\ntransition band and attenuation.<\/li>\n<li>Why can 650 Hz and 150 Hz not be distinguished even with an\narbitrarily long FFT of the same ideal samples?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> The time sequences are identical; the same\nlinear transformation produces the same spectra.<\/li>\n<li>At a sampling rate of 1 kHz, the timing uncertainty is 1 µs.\nEstimate the maximum amplitude error of a 180 Hz sine wave with a peak\namplitude of 1 V. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|dx/dt|\\le2\\pi fA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately 1.13 mV follows; this is a linear approximation for small\njitter.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Communication, Foundation Core (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-161-signal-processing-continuous-and-discrete-fall-2008/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 2.161 Signal Processing: Continuous and Discrete,\nLecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-341-discrete-time-signal-processing-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 6.341 Discrete-Time Signal Processing, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M25-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M25-L2 – Fourier and z-Transforms: Describing Discrete-Time Systems and Filters<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor–Master Bridge: Transition to Digital Signal Processing and Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Bachelor–Master Bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M5-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the frequency\nresponse, stability and transfer function of simple discrete-time\nfilters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From sequence to spectrum<\/h2>\n<p>For an absolutely summable sequence, the discrete-time Fourier\ntransform (DTFT) is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(e^{j\\Omega})=\\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty}x[n]e^{-j\\Omega n}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It is continuous in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-periodic.\nIn contrast, the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of a finite sequence\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nelements calculates a finite number of values:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X[k]=\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x[n]e^{-j2\\pi kn/N},\\qquad x[n]=\\frac1N\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}X[k]e^{j2\\pi kn/N}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The FFT is an algorithm for calculating the DFT, not a different\ntransform. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=1000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe spacing of the frequency points is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz. A 125 Hz oscillation lies at index 25. At 127 Hz, the oscillation\nlies between two points; the rectangular observation window causes\nspectral leakage. A Hann window reduces sidelobes but widens the main\nlobe. Zero padding produces more interpolated spectral points, but no\nadditional measurement information.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Convolution and transfer function<\/h2>\n<p>A linear time-invariant (LTI) system is described by its impulse\nresponse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith zero initial conditions,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=\\sum_{m=-\\infty}^{\\infty}h[m]x[n-m].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the frequency domain, convolution becomes multiplication:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y(e^{j\\Omega})=H(e^{j\\Omega})X(e^{j\\Omega})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA causal moving average over four samples has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[0..3]=1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts transfer function is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)=\\frac{1+z^{-1}+z^{-2}+z^{-3}}4.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>On the unit circle,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(e^{j\\Omega})=e^{-j3\\Omega/2}\\sin(2\\Omega)/(4\\sin(\\Omega/2))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith a limiting value of one at DC. Zeros at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuppress the frequencies 250 Hz and 500 Hz at a sampling rate of 1 kHz.\nThe group delay in the passband is 1.5 samples or 1.5 ms. Blockwise DFT\nmultiplication initially yields circular convolution; linear convolution\nof two finite sequences of lengths\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires a transform length of at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N+M-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000219_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Impulse response and magnitude frequency response for FIR and IIR examples.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Impulse response and magnitude frequency\nresponse for FIR and IIR examples.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>z-Transform and stability<\/h2>\n<p>The bilateral z-transform is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X(z)=\\sum x[n]z^{-n}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is uniquely specified only together with a region of convergence. For\nthe recursive system<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=0.8y[n-1]+0.2x[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>with zero initial conditions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z)=0.2/(1-0.8z^{-1})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe causal impulse response\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h[n]=0.2\\cdot0.8^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis absolutely summable; the pole is at 0.8 inside the unit circle. The\nregion of convergence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|z|&gt;0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains the unit circle. The system is therefore BIBO-stable. In\ngeneral, for causal rational systems after actual pole-zero\ncancellations have been removed, all poles must have magnitudes less\nthan one. In the presence of hidden states, the transfer function alone\ndoes not necessarily establish internal stability.<\/p>\n<p>For a unit step beginning at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe response is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y[n]=1-0.8^{n+1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt least 95% of the final value is reached after 14 samples\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nAn FIR filter has a finite impulse response and is BIBO-stable for\nfinite coefficients; an IIR filter can provide strong smoothing at lower\norder but is sensitive to feedback and coefficient errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selection according to requirements<\/h2>\n<p>A filter is not necessarily better simply because its residual\namplitude is lower. For a measurement signal, passband error,\ninterference attenuation, phase response, delay and computational effort\ncan be competing requirements. The four-point FIR uses four values and\neliminates the 250 Hz component exactly in the ideal model. The IIR\nneeds only the previous output and the current sample but has\nfrequency-dependent group delay. Rounding may produce residual errors in\nfixed-point implementations; limit cycles in recursive filters must be\ntested separately. The frequency response shown here is a model with\nreal coefficients, not a claim about any particular hardware.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkHz, 1000 samples. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta f=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHz, observation duration 0.5 s.<\/li>\n<li>Determine the first three values of the moving average in response\nto a unit step with zero prior history. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 0.25;\n0.5; 0.75. From the fourth sample onward, the value is one.<\/li>\n<li>Replace the IIR pole at 0.8 with one at 1.02, keeping the numerator\nunchanged. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The causal impulse response grows,\nis not absolutely summable and the system is not BIBO-stable.<\/li>\n<li>Why does doubling the FFT length by adding zeros not halve the\nphysical resolution interval? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> The observation\ntime remains the same. Only the frequency grid becomes denser.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Communication, Foundation Core (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-161-signal-processing-continuous-and-discrete-fall-2008/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 2.161 Signal Processing: Continuous and Discrete,\nLecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-341-discrete-time-signal-processing-fall-2005/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 6.341 Discrete-Time Signal Processing, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M25-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M25-L3 – Random Signals, Correlation and Noise: From Model to Measurable Quantity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor–Master Bridge: Transition to Digital Signal Processing and Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Bachelor–Master Bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L2<\/a>;\nsupplementary University Mathematics <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L7<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_mathematics&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L9<\/a>\naccording to the curriculum.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine the mean, variance,\nautocorrelation and noise power from models and data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Random variable and random process<\/h2>\n<p>A random variable describes an uncertain numerical value. A\ndiscrete-time random process\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes a sequence of such variables. Each realisation is a specific\nmeasurement sequence; different realisations can look different while\nfollowing the same model. The mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu[n]=E[X[n]]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2[n]=E[(X[n]-\\mu[n])^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncharacterise the first two moments.<\/p>\n<p>A real wide-sense stationary process (WSS) has a constant finite mean\nand an autocorrelation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_X[k]=E[X[n]X[n-k]]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat depends only on the time difference. The autocovariance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_X[k]=R_X[k]-\\mu^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStationarity does not guarantee ergodicity; time averages from a single\nrealisation may be interpreted as estimators of ensemble means only\nunder suitable conditions.<\/p>\n<p>For a finite sample\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x[0],\\ldots,x[N-1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mean and variance can be estimated using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=\\sum x[n]/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=\\sum (x[n]-\\bar x)^2/(N-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second expression is an unbiased variance estimator under i.i.d.\nassumptions. With temporal correlation, this property may be lost. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le k&lt;N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na simple autocovariance estimator is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>C<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat C[k]=\\frac1N\\sum_{n=k}^{N-1}(x[n]-\\bar x)(x[n-k]-\\bar x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At large lags, fewer products are available. The factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nintroduces a boundary bias; dividing by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N-k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremoves the corresponding factor when the mean is known, but may\nincrease the variance. Both variants must be interpreted with caution\nwhen the mean is estimated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>White noise and power spectral density<\/h2>\n<p>Zero-mean white noise has autocorrelation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_W[k]=\\sigma_w^2\\delta[k]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhite means uncorrelated, not necessarily Gaussian or independent. If\nthe random variables are jointly Gaussian, absence of correlation\nimplies independence. The power spectral density is the DTFT of the\nautocorrelation (the Wiener–Khinchin relation for WSS):<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><mo accent=\"false\">∞<\/mo><\/munderover><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>π<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_X(e^{j\\Omega})=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}R_X[k]e^{-j\\Omega k},\\qquad R_X[0]=\\frac1{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}S_X(e^{j\\Omega})d\\Omega.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For white noise,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_W=\\sigma_w^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this normalised frequency convention. In physical hertz, the\ntwo-sided density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_f(f)=S_X(e^{j2\\pi f/f_s})/f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith units such as V²/Hz. A one-sided spectrum of a real signal doubles\nthe density at positive frequencies (excluding DC and, where applicable,\nthe Nyquist point of a DFT).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coloured noise as a process model<\/h2>\n<p>A simple autoregressive model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X[n]=aX[n-1]+W[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a|&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand independent innovations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W[n]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith zero mean and variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_w^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a stationary solution, the variance equation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x^2=a^2\\sigma_x^2+\\sigma_w^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand hence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x^2=\\frac{\\sigma_w^2}{1-a^2},\\quad R_X[k]=\\sigma_x^2a^{|k|},\\quad S_X(e^{j\\Omega})=\\frac{\\sigma_w^2}{|1-ae^{-j\\Omega}|^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_w=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe variance is one; the normalised correlation at lag 2 is 0.64.\nCompared with white noise of the same variance, adjacent samples are\noften similar. The figure uses synthetic realisations with the initial\ntransient discarded; it is not a measurement from a real sensor.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000220_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic white noise, an AR(1) process and empirical and theoretical autocorrelation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic white noise, an AR(1) process\nand empirical and theoretical autocorrelation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Filtering and measurement uncertainty<\/h2>\n<p>For a stable LTI system,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>Y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S_Y=|H|^2 S_X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor independent samples with variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\naveraging over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsamples reduces the variance to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2/M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor correlated samples,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{1}{M^2}\\left(MR_X[0]+2\\sum_{k=1}^{M-1}(M-k)R_X[k]\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>is needed for a zero-mean stationary signal. Four independent samples\nwith unit variance yield 0.25; for the AR(1) model above, the result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.64<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.512<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.774<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4+2(3\\cdot0.8+2\\cdot0.64+0.512))/16=0.774<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAveraging can therefore be considerably less effective for coloured\nnoise. An additional DC component shifts the mean but is not eliminated\nby averaging. When specifying SNR, the bandwidth must be documented:\nsignal power and noise power must be determined for the same measurement\nchain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Noise of 2 mV RMS is averaged over nine independent samples.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 0.667 mV RMS.<\/li>\n<li>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe desired variance is one. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Innovation\nvariance 0.75; standard deviation approximately 0.866.<\/li>\n<li>How does an empirical correlation curve differ from a theoretical\none? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Finite samples fluctuate; mean\nestimation and boundary treatment affect the values, and model\ndeviations are also possible.<\/li>\n<li>A sinusoidal signal component has 1 V RMS and the noise has 10 mV\nRMS. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>0.01<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\log_{10}(1^2/0.01^2)=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB under the same impedance and bandwidth.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Communication, Foundation Core (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/2-161-signal-processing-continuous-and-discrete-fall-2008/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 2.161 Signal Processing: Continuous and Discrete,\nLecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 6.450 Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M25-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M25-L4 – Digital Transmission: Baseband, Symbol Decisions and Intersymbol Interference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor–Master Bridge: Transition to Digital Signal Processing and Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Bachelor–Master Bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive symbol decisions from a\nsignal model and distinguish different sources of errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From bit to received symbol<\/h2>\n<p>A binary antipodal baseband model maps bits\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_k\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto symbols\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_k=2b_k-1\\in\\{-1,+1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA pulse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith unit energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int |p(t)|^2dt=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nshapes the signal<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/munder><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(t)=\\sqrt{E_b}\\sum_k a_k p(t-kT).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the energy per bit and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the symbol interval. The corresponding modulated BPSK can be modelled\nas a projection onto a real carrier basis function. Only the equivalent\nreal baseband dimension is considered here. With one bit per symbol, the\nbit rate and symbol rate are equal; this is not generally the case for\nother modulation schemes.<\/p>\n<p>A filter matched to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(matched filter) correlates the received signal with the pulse. In\nadditive white Gaussian noise with two-sided power spectral density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit maximises the signal-to-noise ratio at the decision instant for an\nisolated pulse. If the shifted pulses are orthogonal, or if there is no\nISI at the sampling instant, the observed real quantity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_k=\\sqrt{E_b}a_k+w_k,\\qquad w_k\\sim\\mathcal N(0,N_0/2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Decision rule and theoretical error rate<\/h2>\n<p>For equally probable bits, symmetric costs and known amplitude, a\nzero threshold minimises the probability of error. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_k\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe decision is bit 1; otherwise it is bit 0. The conditional\nprobability of error for the positive symbol is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msqrt><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(w_k&lt;-\\sqrt{E_b})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the Gaussian tail probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msubsup><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\int_x^\\infty e^{-u^2/2}du<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">erfc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b=Q\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2E_b}{N_0}}\\right)=\\frac12\\operatorname{erfc}\\left(\\sqrt{E_b/N_0}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(0 dB), the result is approximately 0.07865; at 6 dB it is approximately\n0.00239. Noise variance in this real normalised model: set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=10^{\\mathrm{dB}/10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=1/\\sqrt{2\\gamma}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConfusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nintroduces a factor of two in the variance, corresponding to a 3 dB\ndiscrepancy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Intersymbol interference (ISI)<\/h2>\n<p>A dispersive channel or non-ideal pulse can cause neighbouring\nsymbols to overlap at the decision instant. The model of the effective\nchannel chain, after sampling, is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_k=a_k+\\alpha a_{k-1}+w_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe noise-free levels are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sign of the symbol decision remains correct, but for opposite\npreceding symbols the distance to the threshold decreases from one to\n0.5. Errors now depend on the symbol sequence. For i.i.d. equiprobable\nbits and independent Gaussian noise samples, with the same\nzero-threshold detector, the result is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_b(\\alpha)=\\tfrac12 Q((1+\\alpha)/\\sigma)+\\tfrac12 Q((1-\\alpha)/\\sigma).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 6 dB, the model value rises to 0.03958. This relation applies to the\nspecified channel and detector, not to arbitrary radio channels. Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to the input symbol energy; the channel is not energy-normalised\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges. An exact physical comparison requires calibration of the\neffective channel and noise models.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000221_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four possible noise-free received levels of the two-tap channel and noisy signal distributions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four possible noise-free received levels\nof the two-tap channel and noisy signal distributions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pulse shaping and equalisation<\/h2>\n<p>A Nyquist pulse shape has zero crossings at integer symbol intervals\naway from its main pulse. A raised-cosine overall filter satisfies this\ncondition under ideal synchronisation; the real baseband bandwidth is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+\\beta)/(2T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor roll-off\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le\\beta\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRoot-raised-cosine filters are often used at both the transmitter and\nreceiver. An equaliser can reduce ISI after channel estimation; however,\na zero-forcing equaliser also amplifies noise in regions of low channel\ngain. A decision-feedback equaliser can subtract preceding symbols, but\nrequires correct previous decisions and may propagate errors.<\/p>\n<p>In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L5<\/a>, pulse\nshaping, timing and carrier synchronisation remain idealised. Only the\nreal discrete channel at the optimal decision instant is investigated.\nThis makes it possible to verify the theoretical BER without pretending\nto model a complete RF system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>A BPSK transmitter transmits 100 kbit/s. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n100 ksymbols/s,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nµs; with an ideal raised-cosine overall filter and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe one-sided baseband bandwidth is 62.5 kHz.<\/li>\n<li><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_k=+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{k-1}=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Noise-free received value 0.7.<\/li>\n<li>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhat is the standard deviation? <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>4<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt4=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor normalised\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/li>\n<li>Why does higher RF transmit power alone not guarantee correct symbol\ndecisions? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> ISI, synchronisation errors,\nnon-linearity, interference and other errors outside the model may\nremain; power is only one part of system design.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication, Foundation Core (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 6.450 Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M25-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M25-L5 – Practical Assignment: Simulation of Binary Transmission and Comparison with the Theoretical Bit Error Rate<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M25 – Bachelor–Master Bridge: Transition to Digital Signal Processing and Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Bachelor–Master Bridge<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L1<\/a> to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Reproduce a computational study,\ncheck it against an analytical reference and recognise the limits of\nstatistical conclusions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assignment and reference model<\/h2>\n<p>Create a reproducible experiment for binary antipodal transmission\nwith real AWGN and optional intersymbol interference. Use uniformly\ndistributed independent bits, a zero threshold, unit input symbol energy\nand an ideally known sampling instant. Compare four values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0=0,3,6,9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB for each of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(AWGN) and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(discrete two-tap channel). Exclude the first symbol from the evaluation\nbecause of the artificial zero initial history. A total of 999,999 valid\nbit decisions per case are considered.<\/p>\n<p>The independent analytical reference value comes from the Gaussian\ndistribution: for pairs of current and previous symbols, the distances\nto the threshold are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach with probability 50%. Therefore, the formula from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L4&amp;section=S3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L4<\/a>\napplies. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit reduces to the BPSK-AWGN formula. At 9 dB without ISI, the\ntheoretical BER is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.36<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.36\\cdot10^{-5}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non average, about 34 errors are expected in one million bits. This\nillustrates why very small error rates require very large numbers of\nbits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Executable experiment<\/h2>\n<p>The program requires Python 3 and NumPy. <code>default_rng<\/code>\nuses a fixed seed. The same bit and noise sequences are reused for all\ncases to facilitate comparison; the cases are therefore not independent.\nThe noise samples themselves are independent across symbol times. The\ntheoretical error rate is compared with the empirical rate, not with a\nsecond simulation using the same model equations.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> erfc, sqrt<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">2505<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>N<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1_000_000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rng.integers(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,N)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rng.standard_normal(N)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>prev<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.r_[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,a[:<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> db <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    gamma<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(db<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> sigma<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>gamma)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> alpha <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>alpha<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>prev<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>sigma<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>z<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"co\"># exclude first symbol (zero initial history)<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        errors<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.count_nonzero((y[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span>b[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x:<span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>erfc(x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        theory<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(q((<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>alpha)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sigma)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q((<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>alpha)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sigma))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        se<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>sqrt(theory<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>theory)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(errors<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>theory)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>se<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(db,alpha,errors,n,<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>errors<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>n<span class=\"sc\">:.8f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>theory<span class=\"sc\">:.8f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The final test uses a broad statistical plausibility band based on\nthe theoretical Bernoulli standard deviation and one additional\nerror-count increment. It is not an exact confidence bound, a\ncertification test or proof for an arbitrary channel model. For very\nsmall expected error counts, discrete binomial or Poisson methods are\nmore suitable. A systematic confidence analysis must consider additional\nindependent seeds and the dependence between decision errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results of the reference run<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(dB)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Errors<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Simulated BER<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Theory<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">78725<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.07872508<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.07864960<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">128262<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.12826213<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.12834874<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">23148<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.02314802<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.02287841<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80245<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.08024508<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.08015396<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2436<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00243600<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00238829<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">39571<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.03957104<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.03957699<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">29<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00002900<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00003363<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11744<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.01174401<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.01156816<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000222_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Eight simulated and theoretical bit error rates for AWGN and a two-tap channel at four energy-to-noise ratios.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Eight simulated and theoretical bit error\nrates for AWGN and a two-tap channel at four energy-to-noise\nratios.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The values are interpreted as dimensionless fractions rather than\npercentages. At 6 dB, the result is 0.2436% without ISI and 3.9571% with\nISI. In both model cases, the measured error rate decreases as the input\nenergy relative to the noise power spectral density increases. With ISI,\nthe decrease is slower; even a correct threshold is no substitute for a\nsuitable equaliser. At 9 dB, only 29 errors remain without ISI; relative\ndeviations become large for small error counts. The simulation must\ntherefore be assessed on the basis of absolute statistical variation,\nnot agreement to the same decimal places.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validation and extension<\/h2>\n<p>First perform a deterministic check: without noise samples, every\nsymbol must be decided correctly for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\alpha|&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_kr_k=1+\\alpha a_ka_{k-1}&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe theoretical expression and the scalar AWGN formula must agree. As a\nfurther check, the four symbol pairs can be counted and the two\nconditional error rates evaluated separately. The empirical noise\nvariance is checked against\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2\\gamma)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the test model, each valid bit decision is counted exactly once; if\nthe initial history changes, the evaluation range must be adjusted\naccordingly.<\/p>\n<p>Optionally extend the model with an ideal detector that knows the\npreceding symbol and subtracts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha a_{k-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbefore the threshold detector. This yields exactly the AWGN model. It is\nan ideal reference for equalisation with an error-free history, not a\nrealisable detector without additional knowledge. A real\ndecision-feedback equaliser uses its own preceding decision and can\ntherefore cause subsequent errors. The difference from the ideal\nreference is a useful metric. The model includes no frequency offsets,\nphase errors, oversampling, real channel impulse response, coloured\nnoise, RF power calibration or channel coding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practical assignment and transition to the Master’s\nlevel<\/h2>\n<p>Prepare a short report with a parameter table, Python/NumPy version,\nseed, error count, number of evaluated bits, analytical reference value,\nBER plot and model limitations. Increase the sample size or repeat with\nnew seeds if the error count is below 100 and greater estimation\naccuracy is required. If necessary, store the counts instead of only\nrounded BER values. In this course, the bridge is considered complete\nwhen you can work through the following tasks yourself:<\/p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Explain aliasing and choose a sampling rate (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L1<\/a>).<\/li>\n<li>Determine a digital filter from its impulse response or difference\nequation (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L2<\/a>).<\/li>\n<li>Relate noise variance and autocorrelation to model assumptions (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L3<\/a>).<\/li>\n<li>Derive a decision rule and its error rate (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L4<\/a>).<\/li>\n<li>Compare the simulation and reference calculation, stating the\nuncertainty.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Check question:<\/strong> What does zero observed errors in\none million independent bits mean? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> Not zero\nBER. The one-sided 95% upper bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.05<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.05^{1/10^6}\\approx3\\cdot10^{-6}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder independent, identically distributed error indicators with\nconstant error probability. For dependent errors, this evaluation does\nnot apply without further assumptions.<\/p>\n<p>The Master’s specialisation M30 goes beyond the error rate to\nentropy, coding, capacity and optical transmission. The other bridges\nM21–M24 open up alternative Master’s profiles; they are not replaced by\na single BER experiment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=1&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication, Foundation Core (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<li><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-450-principles-of-digital-communication-i-fall-2009/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOpenCourseWare – 6.450 Principles of Digital Communication I, Lecture\nNotes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
